<!-- HERO -->
<div class="about-hero">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="about-hero-text about-lede">
            <div class="label">About me</div>
            <h1>Diogo<br>Serino</h1>
            <p class="about-hero-role">
                <span>Builder &amp; generalist.</span> I work end to end, across product, data and design.
            </p>
            <p>I'm Diogo. I build products from one end to the other: the idea, the data underneath, the interface, the version that actually ships. The part I like most is when a fuzzy problem finally turns into something you can click.</p>
            <p>My path hasn't been a straight line. I've been a freelance designer, a financial analyst, and now I build my own product. For years I read that as a problem, too many directions and no clean title to write on a form. I've come round to the opposite view.</p>
            <p>I don't think in departments. A product decision is usually a data question and a design question at the same time, and I'd rather hold all three at once than hand each one off and hope they line up at the end.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>


<!-- THE LONG WAY ROUND -->
<section class="about-section" id="path">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="about-section-header">
            <div class="label">How I got here</div>
            <h2>The long way round</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="about-prose">
            <p>I spent about seven years as a freelance designer before I called myself anything to do with product. Brand identities, websites, 3D interior renders, whatever the client in front of me needed. Somewhere in there I built a clothing brand of my own, <strong>Dimser</strong>, from the logo to the online store. That was the first time I owned a whole thing end to end, and I got a taste for it.</p>
            <p>Then I went into finance. As a Financial Analyst at a multi-company real-estate group, I didn't just read the dashboards. I built them. Power BI, Python, SQL, KPI frameworks: the reports I made replaced the manual ones and became what people actually used to decide where the money went.</p>
            <p>Different rooms, same job. Design taught me to build things people use. Finance taught me to build things people trust with a real decision. Product is where those two meet, so that's where I ended up, building instead of waiting for someone to hand me the brief.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>


<!-- WHAT I'VE BUILT -->
<section class="about-section section-alt" id="work">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="about-section-header">
            <div class="label">What I make</div>
            <h2>Things I've built</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="proj-list">

            <div class="proj fade-up">
                <div class="proj-title">TryCareerMatch</div>
                <p>A free, no-signup career-discovery app I built solo, full-stack. It maps 120+ skills to 60+ roles across 9 sectors, names the hybrid archetype your profile points to, and ranks real roles by fit. The scoring is rule-based and runs on the server. There's no LLM inside the product, and that was deliberate: I wanted logic I could explain, not a black box that sounds clever.</p>
                <a class="proj-link" href="https://trycareermatch.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trycareermatch.com</a>
            </div>

            <div class="proj fade-up">
                <div class="proj-title">The writing</div>
                <p>25+ articles on product, analytics, growth and strategy, all here on this site. I write them partly to explain things clearly to other people and partly to find the gaps in my own understanding. You learn a topic properly the day you have to write it down.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="proj fade-up">
                <div class="proj-title">Consulting for small businesses</div>
                <p>Freelance work for SMEs around Braga and Porto: business plans, financial analysis, viability studies. Smaller in scale than a product, but it keeps me close to real numbers and the decisions that ride on them.</p>
            </div>

        </div>
    </div>
</section>


<!-- GENERALIST -->
<section class="about-section" id="generalist">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="about-section-header">
            <div class="label">Where I fit</div>
            <h2>A generalist, on purpose</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="about-prose">
            <p>That's the label I've made peace with. I like learning across subjects, I get restless without a new problem to chew on, and I'm at my best when a job needs a bit of everything. A non-linear career used to feel like a liability. Now it's just how I'm built, and it happens to be the kind of range early-stage teams actually need.</p>
            <p>I'm an active member of <strong>Generalist World</strong>, a community for people wired to do a lot of things rather than one. It runs a newsletter and a Slack, and I'm in there most weeks: joining the discussions, writing, and trying to be useful to people working through the same question I did. What do you do when "pick one thing" was never good advice for you?</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>


